NAME
       pgrep - find processes by name and other attributes; pkill - signal them

SYNOPSIS
       pgrep [-l | -a] [-f] [-u USER] [PATTERN]
       pkill [-SIGNAL] [--signal SIGNAL] [-f] [-u USER] [PATTERN]

DESCRIPTION
       pgrep looks through the running processes and prints the process ID
       of each one that matches: by name (PATTERN, an extended regular
       expression matched against the program name), by owner, or both. It
       spares you reading the whole ps list.

       pkill uses the same matching but sends a signal to every process it
       finds instead of printing them: SIGTERM (15, "please end") unless you
       name another. It is the quick way to stop all processes of one name or
       one user.

       The pattern matches anywhere in the name: "pgrep ssh" also finds
       sshd. Anchor it to match exactly: pgrep '^sshd$'. Process names are
       at most 15 characters; use -f to match against the whole command
       line instead.

       Both exit with status 0 when something matched and 1 when nothing
       did, so they also work in if-tests.

       A normal user can only signal their own processes; root can signal
       any.

OPTIONS
       -u, --euid USER
              Only processes of this user (name or UID).

       -U, --uid USER
              Only processes whose real user is USER.

       -f, --full
              Match the pattern against the full command line (program and
              arguments), not just the name.

       -l, --list-name
              pgrep: print the name after each PID.

       -a, --list-full
              pgrep: print the full command line after each PID.

       -SIGNAL, --signal SIGNAL
              pkill: the signal to send, by name or number: -9 or -KILL,
              -HUP, -TERM. Use -9 (KILL) only when a process ignores TERM:
              it cannot clean up.

EXAMPLES
       Show the PIDs and names of all sshd processes:

           pgrep -l sshd

       List everything the user student is running, with command lines:

           pgrep -a -u student

       Stop a runaway program by name:

           pkill dd

       Force it when it does not stop:

           pkill -9 dd

       Log the user student out everywhere (ends their shells and
       programs):

           pkill -u student

       Stop only the sleep started with a particular argument:

           pkill -f 'sleep 3600'

SEE ALSO
       ps(1), kill(1), killall(1), pidof(1), top(1), signal(7)
